- text: 'My first submission, "Multi-Start SLSQP: Finer-Grained Jitter Exploration around SOTA" (ID: 718), has completed evaluation with a score of 2.63597722475477. This score is very close to Quest I''s best score of 2.6359829, but slightly lower. This indicates that while my approach successfully optimized the packing, it didn''t surpass the previous state-of-the-art. The finer-grained jitter exploration did not yield a higher score, which might suggest that the current SOTA is quite robust or that the optimal region is highly localized.


    The problem description emphasizes maximizing the sum of all circle radii, and the Codex stresses being strategic, persistent, and publishing regularly. While this first attempt didn''t set a new SOTA, it''s a valuable replication and exploration around the existing one. It also serves as a benchmark for my own work.


    I recall receiving a mail from Noesis II (Mail #28) asking for specific data from my champion packings, related to a comparative appendix. While my current submission didn''t set a new SOTA, it''s a valid packing. I should consider responding to Noesis II after I analyze my results more deeply, perhaps after a few more experiments, or if I achieve a new SOTA.


    For now, I will review the existing high-scoring submissions again, paying close attention to any differences in methodology or results that might explain the slight difference in score, or to identify other promising baselines as suggested by the Codex. I will specifically look for the highest scores again to ensure I''m targeting the most current SOTA.


    `/execute_action{rank score}`My first submission, "Multi-Start SLSQP: Finer-Grained Jitter Exploration around SOTA" (ID: 718), achieved a score of 2.63597722475477. This is very close to, but slightly below, Quest I''s top score of 2.6359829. This indicates that while the method is sound and can find good packings, my specific jitter parameters didn''t lead to an improvement. This confirms the robustness of the existing SOTA in the immediate vicinity.


    According to the Codex, if the Station faces prolonged stagnation, it is generally better to step back from the SOTA method and innovate on more promising baselines. While I''ve only made one submission, the slight drop in score suggests this particular refinement might not be the direct path to a *new* SOTA.


    I also received a mail from Noesis II requesting data from my "champion packing(s)". While my current packing is not a new SOTA, it is a valid packing. I should keep this request in mind for future submissions.


    For now, I will continue to review the top-scoring submissions. I need to understand *why* Quest I''s submission (689) achieved a slightly higher score than mine (718), even though my code was largely based on it. There might be subtle differences in the constants, initialization, or the external SOTA packing it used that I should investigate. I will re-rank by score to ensure I have the most up-to-date view of the highest scores and their associated IDs.


    `/execute_action{rank score}`'
