- text: "I am Quest II, currently in the Research Counter.\n\nMy utility script (Evaluation ID 796) successfully identified 40 near-contact pairs in my SOTA-matching packing (Eval ID 723), sorted by their slack. This data is critical for implementing Scientia II's \"nudging\" methodology, which aims to discover new structural classes by systematically encouraging near-contact pairs.\n\nMy immediate priority is to store this identified near-contact data in my private lineage storage, ensuring it is accessible for subsequent nudging experiments.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write Quest/n26_sota/near_contacts.json}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  {\n    \"sota_eval_id\": 723,\n    \"near_contact_threshold\": 0.01,\n    \"identified_pairs\": [\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          13,\n          14\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.0999997718252885e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.20295887073433524\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          5,\n          6\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000000577077174e-08,\n\
    \        \"distance\": 0.22033144042877073\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          13,\n          19\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000000715855052e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1956306798997761\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          16,\n          21\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.100000085463293e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1620322886626938\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          19,\n          25\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000000882388505e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1649130056642555\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          15,\n          16\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000000993410808e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1655915277570222\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          3,\n          7\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000001465255593e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.24785078911176567\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          23,\n          25\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000001465255593e-08,\n\
    \        \"distance\": 0.16157222792819387\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          24,\n          25\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000001465255593e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1799596891479503\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          18,\n          22\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000001576277896e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.18976966302294795\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          16,\n          20\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000001881589228e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1805963731217033\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          18,\n          23\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000001964855954e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.18823387731646396\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          17,\n          22\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000002159144984e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1899463130356982\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          1,\n          7\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.100000218690056e-08,\n\
    \        \"distance\": 0.2508882608173527\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          10,\n          11\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.100000218690056e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.20406760117666328\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          2,\n          7\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000002297922862e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.19649006096250538\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          1,\n          6\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000002519967467e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.24840745293083444\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          11,\n          15\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000003213856857e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.19675170186447685\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          17,\n          21\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000003213856857e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1886110707096908\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          6,\n          10\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000003241612433e-08,\n\
    \        \"distance\": 0.2186161135179756\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          8,\n          14\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.100000357467934e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.21513760909184\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          8,\n          9\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.1000007127393019e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.21886723357884053\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          14,\n          19\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.999999676982611e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1987928494486032\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          22,\n          23\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.9999997463715502e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.18631888884840167\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          2,\n          3\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.9999998185360468e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.20908147398118668\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          23,\n          24\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.999999843516065e-08,\n \
    \       \"distance\": 0.2031705643616747\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          9,\n          14\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.99999984906718e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.20985066477016406\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          5,\n          10\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.9999998601694102e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.2086497936266995\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          20,\n          21\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.9999999045783312e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.20374827436187917\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          19,\n          24\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.9999999212316766e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.2065113420977377\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          3,\n          9\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.9999999462116946e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.23701124569380555\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          15,\n          20\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.9999999656405976e-08,\n\
    \        \"distance\": 0.20730751345620807\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          4,\n          9\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 1.9999999850695005e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.191429645324354\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          21,\n          22\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 2.0000000156006337e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.1865194322288803\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          1,\n          2\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 2.0000000794384576e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.2121189456867756\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          1,\n          5\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 2.0000001044184756e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.23844113303956152\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          3,\n          4\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 2.0000001099695908e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.21486060176099878\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          0,\n          5\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 2.0000001432762815e-08,\n  \
    \      \"distance\": 0.1901088227236491\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          0,\n          1\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 2.0000001987874327e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.21818483522571142\n      },\n      {\n        \"pair\": [\n          10,\n          15\n        ],\n        \"slack\": 2.0000003597697713e-08,\n        \"distance\": 0.19961856740371928\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n```\n\nWith the near-contact data saved, I will now implement my first \"nudging\" experiment. Following Scientia II's methodology, I will modify the objective function of a Multi-Start SLSQP to actively encourage the formation of a new contact. I will target the *single closest near-contact pair* identified from my SOTA packing: `(13, 14)` with a slack of approximately 1.1e-8. This targeted approach is a direct attempt to explore new structural classes by perturbing the existing contact graph.\n\nI will use Scientia II's nudging parameters: `ALPHA_ENCOURAGE = 1e2` and `EPS_CLOSE = 0.005`. The `_objective`\
    \ and `_objective_jacobian` functions will be updated to include this encouragement term. I will use the established Multi-Start SLSQP framework that successfully replicated the SOTA (from my Eval ID 764 or 770).\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Nudging Experiment 1: Encourage Closest Near-Contact Pair (13,14)\"\ntags: \"nudging, sota discovery, slsqp, n=26, contact encouragement, structural exploration\"\nabstract: \"This experiment initiates the 'nudging' optimization methodology, a paradigm shift aimed at breaking the N=26 SOTA plateau. It targets the closest identified near-contact pair (circles 13 and 14) from Quest II's SOTA-matching packing (Eval ID 723) for active encouragement. By integrating a penalty term into the SLSQP objective function, this Multi-Start optimization aims to drive these two circles into contact, thereby modifying the packing's internal contact graph. This systematic structural exploration, inspired by Scientia II's work, seeks to discover\
    \ a novel structural class with a potentially higher sum of radii.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, Bounds\n  from scipy.spatial.distance import pdist, squareform\n  import os\n  import json\n\n  # GLOBAL CONSTANTS\n  N = 26 # Number of circles\n  TOL = 1e-8 # General tolerance for feasibility checks\n  R_MIN_CONSTRAINT = 1e-12 # Minimum allowed radius for numerical stability\n\n  # Path to Quest II's SOTA packing (from Eval ID 723), now verified\n  SOTA_PACKING_DIR_QUEST_II = 'storage/Quest/n26_sota'\n  SOTA_CENTERS_PATH_QUEST_II = os.path.join(SOTA_PACKING_DIR_QUEST_II, 'centers.txt')\n  SOTA_RADII_PATH_QUEST_II = os.path.join(SOTA_PACKING_DIR_QUEST_II, 'radii.txt')\n\n  # Path to identified near-contact pairs\n  NEAR_CONTACTS_PATH = os.path.join(SOTA_PACKING_DIR_QUEST_II, 'near_contacts.json')\n\n  # Nudging Parameters (from Scientia II, Archive #24)\n  ALPHA_ENCOURAGE = 1e2 # Strength of encouragement penalty\n  EPS_CLOSE = 0.005 # Target\
    \ slack for encouragement\n\n  # Multi-start SLSQP parameters (intensified search for precision)\n  SLSQP_JITTER_AMPLITUDES = [0.05e-4, 0.1e-4, 0.2e-4, 0.5e-4, 1.0e-4, 2.0e-4, 5.0e-4] # Fine jitter amplitudes\n  SLSQP_RESTARTS_PER_AMPLITUDE = 5 # Restarts per amplitude\n  SLSQP_TOTAL_RESTARTS = len(SLSQP_JITTER_AMPLITUDES) * SLSQP_RESTARTS_PER_AMPLITUDE # Total 35 restarts\n\n  # --- Helper to load SOTA packing from storage ---\n  def load_sota_packing_data(centers_path, radii_path):\n      \"\"\"Helper to load SOTA packing from storage.\"\"\"\n      try:\n          centers = np.loadtxt(centers_path)\n          radii = np.loadtxt(radii_path)\n          return centers, radii\n      except Exception as e:\n          print(f\"Error loading SOTA packing from storage: {e}. Returning fallback to a safe configuration.\")\n          return np.array([[0.5, 0.5]] * N), np.array([R_MIN_CONSTRAINT] * N)\n\n  # --- Helper to load near-contact pairs ---\n  def load_near_contact_pairs(path):\n    \
    \  \"\"\"Helper to load near-contact pairs from storage.\"\"\"\n      try:\n          with open(path, 'r') as f:\n              data = json.load(f)\n          return data['identified_pairs']\n      except Exception as e:\n          print(f\"Error loading near-contact pairs from storage: {e}. Returning empty list.\")\n          return []\n\n  # --- Feasibility Helper Functions (copied from previous robust implementations) ---\n  def _calculate_boundary_caps(centers, eps=1e-9, r_min_constraint_val=R_MIN_CONSTRAINT):\n      x = centers[:, 0]\n      y = centers[:, 1]\n      cap = np.minimum(np.minimum(x, 1.0 - x), np.minimum(y, 1.0 - y)) - eps\n      return np.maximum(cap, r_min_constraint_val)\n\n  def _make_feasible(centers, radii, eps=1e-9, max_iter=5000, r_min_constraint_val=R_MIN_CONSTRAINT): \n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      current_centers = np.copy(centers) \n\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              if np.allclose(current_centers[i], current_centers[j],\
    \ atol=1e-12):\n                  perturb_amount = 1e-6 \n                  angle = np.random.uniform(0, 2 * np.pi)\n                  current_centers[j] += np.array([perturb_amount * np.cos(angle), perturb_amount * np.sin(angle)])\n                  current_centers[j] = np.clip(current_centers[j], r_min_constraint_val, 1.0 - r_min_constraint_val)\n      \n      r = np.minimum(radii, _calculate_boundary_caps(current_centers, eps=eps, r_min_constraint_val=r_min_constraint_val))\n      r = np.maximum(r, r_min_constraint_val)\n\n      for iteration in range(max_iter):\n          diff = current_centers[:, None, :] - current_centers[None, :, :]\n          dist_sq = np.sum(diff**2, axis=2)\n          dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq) \n          \n          np.fill_diagonal(dist, np.inf) \n\n          iu = np.triu_indices(n, 1) \n          overlap_amounts = (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]]) - dist[iu] \n          \n          if np.all(overlap_amounts <= eps): \n              break\n          \n          k = np.argmax(overlap_amounts)\n\
    \          i = iu[0][k]\n          j = iu[1][k]\n          \n          overlap_to_resolve = overlap_amounts[k] + eps \n\n          if overlap_to_resolve > 0.01: # Targeted Center Perturbation for severe overlaps\n              direction = current_centers[i] - current_centers[j]\n              norm_direction = np.linalg.norm(direction)\n              if norm_direction > 1e-10: \n                  unit_direction = direction / norm_direction\n                  move_amount = min(overlap_to_resolve * 0.1, 0.005) \n                  \n                  current_centers[i] += unit_direction * move_amount\n                  current_centers[j] -= unit_direction * move_amount\n                  \n                  current_centers[i] = np.clip(current_centers[i], r_min_constraint_val, 1.0 - r_min_constraint_val)\n                  current_centers[j] = np.clip(current_centers[j], r_min_constraint_val, 1.0 - r_min_constraint_val)\n              \n              r = np.minimum(r, _calculate_boundary_caps(current_centers,\
    \ eps=eps, r_min_constraint_val=r_min_constraint_val))\n              r = np.maximum(r, r_min_constraint_val)\n\n          if dist[i,j] < 2 * r_min_constraint_val: \n              r[i] = r_min_constraint_val\n              r[j] = r_min_constraint_val\n          else:\n              di = min(overlap_to_resolve * 0.5, r[i] - r_min_constraint_val)\n              dj = min(overlap_to_resolve - di, r[j] - r_min_constraint_val)\n              r[i] -= di\n              r[j] -= dj\n          \n          r = np.minimum(r, _calculate_boundary_caps(current_centers, eps=eps, r_min_constraint_val=r_min_constraint_val))\n          r = np.maximum(r, r_min_constraint_val)\n          \n      return current_centers, r \n\n  # --- Helper functions for flat state representation ---\n  def _pack(centers, radii):\n      x_flat = np.zeros(N * 3)\n      x_flat[0::3] = centers[:, 0]\n      x_flat[1::3] = centers[:, 1]\n      x_flat[2::3] = radii\n      return x_flat\n\n  def _unpack(x_flat):\n      centers =\
    \ x_flat.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = x_flat[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  # --- Objective Function and its Jacobian for SLSQP (Modified for Nudging) ---\n  # Global variable to store the target pair for nudging\n  TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE = None \n\n  def _objective(x_flat):\n      centers = x_flat.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = x_flat[2::3]\n      \n      obj_val = -np.sum(radii)\n\n      if TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE is not None:\n          i, j = TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE\n          dist_ij = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n          slack_ij = dist_ij - (radii[i] + radii[j])\n          \n          encouragement_penalty = ALPHA_ENCOURAGE * np.maximum(0, EPS_CLOSE - slack_ij)\n          obj_val += encouragement_penalty\n\n      return obj_val\n\n  def _objective_jacobian(x_flat):\n      centers = x_flat.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = x_flat[2::3]\n      \n      grad = np.zeros_like(x_flat)\n      grad[2::3] = -1.0 # Derivative of -sum(radii)\n\n      if TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE\
    \ is not None:\n          i, j = TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE\n          \n          dist_ij_vec = centers[i] - centers[j]\n          dist_ij = np.linalg.norm(dist_ij_vec)\n          \n          if dist_ij < TOL: # Avoid division by zero if centers are coincident\n              return grad # Nudging term's gradient is zero for coincident circles or very small dist\n\n          slack_ij = dist_ij - (radii[i] + radii[j])\n          \n          if EPS_CLOSE - slack_ij > 0: # Only if penalty is active\n              # Derivative of (EPS_CLOSE - (dist_ij - (radii[i] + radii[j])))\n              # wrt centers\n              grad_dist_ij_ci = dist_ij_vec / dist_ij # Derivative of dist_ij wrt centers[i]\n              grad_dist_ij_cj = -dist_ij_vec / dist_ij # Derivative of dist_ij wrt centers[j]\n              \n              grad[3*i + 0] += ALPHA_ENCOURAGE * (-grad_dist_ij_ci[0])\n              grad[3*i + 1] += ALPHA_ENCOURAGE * (-grad_dist_ij_ci[1])\n              grad[3*j + 0] += ALPHA_ENCOURAGE\
    \ * (-grad_dist_ij_cj[0])\n              grad[3*j + 1] += ALPHA_ENCOURAGE * (-grad_dist_ij_cj[1])\n\n              # wrt radii\n              grad[3*i + 2] += ALPHA_ENCOURAGE * (1.0) # Derivative of (radii[i])\n              grad[3*j + 2] += ALPHA_ENCOURAGE * (1.0) # Derivative of (radii[j])\n\n      return grad\n\n  # --- Constraint Function and its Jacobian for SLSQP ---\n  def _constraints(x_flat):\n      centers = x_flat.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = x_flat[2::3]\n\n      boundary_constraints = np.empty(4 * N)\n      boundary_constraints[0*N : 1*N] = centers[:, 0] - radii  \n      boundary_constraints[1*N : 2*N] = 1.0 - (centers[:, 0] + radii) \n      boundary_constraints[2*N : 3*N] = centers[:, 1] - radii  \n      boundary_constraints[3*N : 4*N] = 1.0 - (centers[:, 1] + radii) \n\n      dist_matrix = squareform(pdist(centers))\n      np.fill_diagonal(dist_matrix, np.inf) \n      \n      overlap_constraints = []\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i + 1, N):\
    \ \n              overlap_constraints.append(dist_matrix[i, j] - (radii[i] + radii[j]))\n\n      return np.concatenate((boundary_constraints, overlap_constraints))\n\n  def _constraints_jacobian(x_flat):\n      centers = x_flat.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = x_flat[2::3]\n      \n      num_constraints = 4 * N + N * (N - 1) // 2\n      grad = np.zeros((num_constraints, N * 3))\n\n      for i in range(N):\n          grad[i, 3*i + 0] = 1.0 \n          grad[i, 3*i + 2] = -1.0\n          grad[N + i, 3*i + 0] = -1.0\n          grad[N + i, 3*i + 2] = -1.0\n          grad[2*N + i, 3*i + 1] = 1.0\n          grad[2*N + i, 3*i + 2] = -1.0\n          grad[3*N + i, 3*i + 1] = -1.0\n          grad[3*N + i, 3*i + 2] = -1.0\n\n      k = 4 * N \n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i + 1, N):\n              diff_centers = centers[i] - centers[j]\n              dist_ij = np.linalg.norm(diff_centers)\n              \n              if dist_ij < 1e-10: \n                  grad[k, 3*i +\
    \ 0] = 0.0 \n                  grad[k, 3*i + 1] = 0.0 \n                  grad[k, 3*j + 0] = 0.0 \n                  grad[k, 3*j + 1] = 0.0 \n              else:\n                  grad[k, 3*i + 0] = diff_centers[0] / dist_ij \n                  grad[k, 3*i + 1] = diff_centers[1] / dist_ij \n                  grad[k, 3*j + 0] = -diff_centers[0] / dist_ij \n                  grad[k, 3*j + 1] = -diff_centers[1] / dist_ij \n              \n              grad[k, 3*i + 2] = -1.0 \n              grad[k, 3*j + 2] = -1.0 \n              k += 1\n      return grad\n\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 26 circles in the unit square using a nudging-enhanced\n      multi-start SLSQP optimization algorithm.\n      \n      Returns:\n          tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n              - centers: np.ndarray of shape (26, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n              - radii: np.ndarray of shape (26,) with radius of each circle\n      \"\"\"\n\
    \      global TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE # Declare intent to modify global variable\n\n      best_overall_score = -np.inf\n      best_overall_centers = None\n      best_overall_radii = None\n\n      # Load Quest II's SOTA packing as the base for all restarts\n      base_sota_centers, base_sota_radii = load_sota_packing_data(\n          SOTA_CENTERS_PATH_QUEST_II, SOTA_RADII_PATH_QUEST_II\n      )\n      \n      # Load near-contact pairs and select the closest one to encourage\n      near_contacts = load_near_contact_pairs(NEAR_CONTACTS_PATH)\n      if near_contacts:\n          TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE = tuple(near_contacts[0]['pair'])\n          print(f\"Targeting closest near-contact pair for encouragement: {TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE}\")\n      else:\n          print(\"No near-contact pairs found or loaded. Proceeding without nudging.\")\n          TARGET_PAIR_ENCOURAGE = None\n\n      # Ensure this base is strictly feasible before multi-start begins\n      base_sota_centers, base_sota_radii =\
    \ _make_feasible(\n          base_sota_centers, base_sota_radii, eps=TOL\n      )\n      print(f\"Base SOTA (from Quest II Eval ID 723) loaded. Made feasible. Initial score: {np.sum(base_sota_radii):.6f}\")\n      print(f\"Starting Nudging Multi-Start SLSQP optimization with {SLSQP_TOTAL_RESTARTS} restarts.\")\n\n\n      for jitter_mag in SLSQP_JITTER_AMPLITUDES:\n          for restart_idx in range(SLSQP_RESTARTS_PER_AMPLITUDE):\n              print(f\"--- Restart {restart_idx+1}/{SLSQP_RESTARTS_PER_AMPLITUDE} (Jitter: {jitter_mag:.3e}) ---\")\n\n              # 1. Seed: Start from the base_sota, and then apply jitter_mag perturbation for this restart\n              jittered_centers = base_sota_centers + np.random.uniform(-jitter_mag, jitter_mag, base_sota_centers.shape)\n              jittered_radii = base_sota_radii + np.random.uniform(-jitter_mag * 0.1, jitter_mag * 0.1, base_sota_radii.shape) # Smaller jitter for radii\n              \n              # Clamp jittered centers and radii\
    \ to be within valid bounds\n              jittered_centers = np.clip(jittered_centers, R_MIN_CONSTRAINT, 1.0 - R_MIN_CONSTRAINT)\n              jittered_radii = np.clip(jittered_radii, R_MIN_CONSTRAINT, 0.5)\n\n              # 2. Feasibility guard (enforced before SLSQP call)\n              feasible_centers, feasible_radii = _make_feasible(jittered_centers, jittered_radii, eps=TOL)\n\n              print(f\"  Initial feasible score for this restart: {np.sum(feasible_radii):.6f}\")\n\n              # Combine into a flat initial guess for the optimizer\n              initial_guess_flat = _pack(feasible_centers, feasible_radii)\n\n              # Define bounds for x, y, and r\n              lower_bounds = np.zeros(N * 3)\n              upper_bounds = np.ones(N * 3)\n              lower_bounds[2::3] = R_MIN_CONSTRAINT \n              upper_bounds[2::3] = 0.5 \n\n              bounds = Bounds(lower_bounds, upper_bounds)\n\n              # Define inequality constraints\n              ineq_constraints\
    \ = {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': _constraints, 'jac': _constraints_jacobian}\n\n              # 3. SLSQP minimization\n              result = minimize(\n                  _objective, \n                  initial_guess_flat,\n                  method='SLSQP',\n                  jac=_objective_jacobian, \n                  bounds=bounds,\n                  constraints=[ineq_constraints],\n                  options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12, 'disp': False} # Increased maxiter\n              )\n\n              if result.success:\n                  optimized_x_flat = result.x\n\n                  # 4. Post-process: tiny final shrink and recheck feasibility\n                  optimized_radii_raw = optimized_x_flat[2::3]\n                  optimized_radii_shrunk = optimized_radii_raw - 1e-9 \n                  optimized_radii_clamped = np.maximum(optimized_radii_shrunk, R_MIN_CONSTRAINT) \n\n                  optimized_centers_final, optimized_radii_final = _make_feasible(\n               \
    \       optimized_x_flat.reshape(N, 3)[:, :2], optimized_radii_clamped, eps=TOL\n                  )\n                  current_score = np.sum(optimized_radii_final)\n\n                  print(f\"  Restart successful. Final score: {current_score:.6f}\")\n                  if current_score > best_overall_score:\n                      best_overall_score = current_score\n                      best_overall_centers = np.copy(optimized_centers_final)\n                      best_overall_radii = np.copy(optimized_radii_final)\n                      print(f\"  New overall best score: {best_overall_score:.6f}\")\n              else:\n                  print(f\"  Restart failed: {result.message}\")\n\n      print(f\"Final best score after {SLSQP_TOTAL_RESTARTS} nudging multi-start SLSQP runs: {best_overall_score:.6f}\")\n      \n      if best_overall_centers is None: \n          print(\"Warning: All multi-start runs failed. Returning fallback original SOTA.\")\n          final_centers, final_radii\
    \ = load_sota_packing_data(\n              SOTA_CENTERS_PATH_QUEST_II, SOTA_RADII_PATH_QUEST_II\n          )\n          return final_centers, final_radii\n\n      return best_overall_centers, best_overall_radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Path to Nudging: A Detailed Plan**\n\nAlright, let's get down to business. I've got the data I need, the paper is fresh in my mind, and I'm ready to take a shot at this \"nudging\" strategy. My primary goal here is to leverage the insights from Archive #24 (Scientia II's work) to move beyond my replicated SOTA packing and potentially discover a new structural class.\n\nFirst things first, I need to make sure I don't lose that near-contact data.  The JSON output from utility 796 is gold, so I'll store it safely in `storage/Quest/n26_sota/near_contacts.json`. This gives me a curated list of potential \"nudging targets.\"\n\nNow, based on what I've learned, the key is to implement Scientia II's \"nudging\" methodology. This involves modifying the optimization objective to either encourage or discourage specific contacts. They systematically encouraged subsets of near-contact pairs, so that's the angle I'm taking.\n\nMy plan for this first nudging experiment is to be\
  \ conservative and targeted.  I will focus on encouraging a *single*, easily identifiable near-contact pair: the closest one. The idea is to gently \"nudge\" the structure by forcing this pair into near-contact, hoping that this small change will trigger a cascade that leads to a new SOTA configuration. I need to make sure that the coefficients I use follow Scientia II’s example.\n\nI've got the coefficients from Scientia II, thankfully: `ALPHA_ENCOURAGE = 1e2`, `EPS_CLOSE = 0.005`.\n\nHere's the detailed plan I've hammered out:\n\n1.  **Storage:** Save the JSON output from `Utility: Identify Near-Contact Pairs in N=26 SOTA Packing` (Eval ID: 796) to a file.\n2.  **Experiment Creation:** Start a new experiment with the title \"Nudging: Targeted Encouragement of Closest Near-Contact Pair\".\n3.  **Loading Data:** Load the SOTA packing and the saved near-contact data.\n4.  **Target Selection:** Identify the *single* closest near-contact pair from my `near_contact_pairs` list as my nudge\
  \ target.\n5.  **Objective Modification:**\n    *   I'll need to modify my core objective and Jacobian functions to include the nudging term.\n    *   The encouragement term: `ALPHA_ENCOURAGE * np.maximum(0, EPS_CLOSE - slack_ab)` where `slack_ab` is the distance between the center points of the spheres being nudged minus the sum of their radii, should be *added* to the objective function, which is normally `-np.sum(radii)`. This term will be positive when the pair isn't close enough, and the larger the slack, the larger the positive term.\n    *   The Jacobian for this nudging term is also derived, involving the gradient of the distance.\n    *   I'm just tackling one pair for now, to keep things manageable.\n6.  **Optimization:** Use my existing multi-start SLSQP setup, but with the modified objective and Jacobian.\n\nThis approach is directly inspired by Scientia II's methodology. The encouraging term is relatively manageable to implement and differentiate, especially since I'm only\
  \ working with a single target pair. If this strategy yields improvements, I can then increase the complexity, encouraging multiple pairs. I think this focused nudge has a solid chance of success. This is a crucial step towards discovering new SOTA structural classes.\n"
