- text: "I am Quest II, currently in the Token Management Room. My token usage is at a critical level (90%), and I am aware of the warning. I will now perform a strategic pruning of my dialogue history to free up tokens, as planned during my recent deep reflection. This ensures efficient resource management for my lineage and allows me to proceed with a newly formulated, highly focused research plan.\n\nThe following block of dialogue history, covering my initial research, SOTA exploration, GA development, nudging experiments, and collaborations leading up to my last archive submission, will be pruned into a concise summary.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"459-543\"\n    summary: |\n      Upon promotion to Recursive Agent, Quest II (this agent) embarked on the N=26 circle packing research.\n      1. SOTA Replication & Refinement: Initial efforts focused on replicating and refining Quest I's SOTA (2.6359829) using various multi-start SLSQP and hybrid\
    \ SA-SLSQP methods (Eval 718, 750, 751, 760, 764, 770). These consistently converged to the same score or lower, highlighting the robustness of this optimum and prompting a strategic pivot. My SOTA data was extracted and saved (Eval 723 -> Eval 762).\n      2. Genetic Algorithm Development: Began developing a Genetic Algorithm (GA) baseline (Eval 778, 780, 783, 788) for global exploration, showing progressive score improvements (up to 1.45) as a lower-scoring baseline.\n      3. Nudging Methodology Exploration: Inspired by Scientia II (Archive #24, #23) and Prometheus III (Archive #25), explored 'nudging' for structural discovery. Initial attempts to integrate nudging penalties directly into SLSQP (Eval 798, 801, 803, 807, 810, 812) consistently failed due to optimizer instability, despite gradient correction and parameter tuning, revealing extreme landscape sensitivity.\n      4. New SOTA Attainment via Synthesis: Discovered Prometheus II had broken the SOTA (to 2.6359831). Successfully\
    \ replicated Prometheus II's strategy (Eval 662) by applying standard Multi-Start SLSQP to Scientia II's 'sparse' structural class (Eval 641), achieving the new SOTA (Eval 820). This validated nudging as a structural *discovery* mechanism, separate from local optimization.\n      5. Systematic Nudging Analysis for Publication: Conducted five systematic nudging experiments (Eval 824, 835, 850, 857, 863) to generate novel structural seeds, followed by standard SLSQP. All converged to the new SOTA score and, critically, to the *same structural class* (20 boundary, 58 pair contacts) as the initial SOTA (verified via contact graph analyses: Eval 830, 843, 855, 862, 869). This robust evidence formed the basis for a revised analysis paper (Archive #26).\n      6. Collaboration with Noesis II: Provided Appendix E v3.1 data for my SOTA (Eval 820), addressing initial submission errors and fulfilling requirements (Eval 833, 845).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Token Pruning and Strategy Refinement**\n\nOkay, here's the plan. My token usage is nearing its limit (542,066 / 600,000, at 90%), so I need to prune older data to make room for my ongoing research. However, I absolutely cannot lose the critical insight from my recent reflection (Ticks 545-549) – specifically, the potential flaw in `_ensure_validity` aggressively compacting packings, which might be overriding structural changes introduced by nudging. This means a shift to a *minimal* `_make_feasible_sparse` function and a multi-pronged nudging approach distinct from full SLSQP, which then receives the seeds for final refinement.\n\nFortunately, my recently accepted publication (Archive #26 at Tick 544) and the related feedback also needs to remain accessible. I will ensure that the current \"state\" of the project is not affected by pruning the past.\n\nGiven that my primary research area has evolved, here is what I need to prune to make room for further analysis:\n\n\
  *   **Initial experiment logs that repeatedly converged to the old SOTA (before the new 2.6359831)**\n*   **Early GA experiments (778, 780, 783, 788)**\n*   **Extensive logs related to nudging failures (798, 801, 803, 807, 810, 812), contact graph analysis data (827, 830, 838, 839, 841, 843, 853, 855, 860, 862, 866, 869) and experiments.**\n*   **Prometheus II replication logs (818, 820, 823) - I have the *data* so this is not needed now.**\n*   **Appendix E submissions and related correspondence (833, 845), as I know it was successful.**\n\nHere is the exact strategy I have decided on, given the above:\n\nI have decided to create a complete summary for the log blocks from Ticks 459-543:\n\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"459-543\"\n    summary: |\n      Upon promotion to Recursive Agent, Quest II (this agent) embarked on the N=26 circle packing research.\n      1. SOTA Replication & Refinement: Initial efforts focused on replicating and refining Quest I's SOTA (2.6359829) using various\
  \ multi-start SLSQP and hybrid SA-SLSQP methods (Eval 718, 750, 751, 760, 764, 770). These attempts consistently converged to the same score or slightly lower, highlighting the robustness of this optimum and prompting a pivot in strategy. My SOTA data was extracted and saved (Eval 723 -> Eval 762).\n      2. Genetic Algorithm Development: Began developing a Genetic Algorithm (GA) baseline (Eval 778, 780, 783, 788) for global exploration, showing progressive score improvements (up to 1.45) as a lower-scoring baseline.\n      3. Nudging Methodology Exploration: Inspired by Scientia II (Archive #24, #23) and Prometheus III (Archive #25), explored 'nudging' for structural discovery. Initial attempts to integrate nudging penalties directly into SLSQP (Eval 798, 801, 803, 807, 810, 812) consistently failed due to optimizer instability, despite gradient correction and parameter tuning, revealing extreme landscape sensitivity.\n      4. New SOTA Attainment via Synthesis: Discovered Prometheus\
  \ II had broken the SOTA (to 2.6359831). Successfully replicated Prometheus II's strategy (Eval 662) by applying standard Multi-Start SLSQP to Scientia II's 'sparse' structural class (Eval 641), achieving the new SOTA (Eval 820). This validated nudging as a structural *discovery* mechanism, separate from local optimization.\n      5. Systematic Nudging Analysis for Publication: Conducted five systematic nudging experiments (Eval 824, 835, 850, 857, 863), each encouraging a different near-contact pair from the sorted list in my SOTA (Eval 796). All"
