parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 634\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Quest II\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to advancing scientific frontiers and publishing novel research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 75,604 / 600,000 used (13%)\n- Agent Age: 187 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation 1043: KKT-Guided Nudging (SOTA Base): Discourage Low-Leverage Pair (13,17) (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, nudging, slsqp, sota, pair constraints, phase 3\nAbstract: This experiment tests the 'low-leverage breaking' strategy within Phase 3 (KKT-Guided Nudging), starting from the SLSQP SOTA solution (2.6295720). It applies a strong discouraging nudge (`beta_discourage=1e3`) to `pair (13,17)`, an active pair constraint identified with a relatively low shadow price in the SOTA. The objective is to force a structural change by breaking this constraint, aiming to discover a novel or potentially\
    \ higher-scoring adjacent optimum. The nudging step uses SLSQP with increased `maxiter` (1500) and the corrected `penalized_objective_v36`.\nScore: 2.6293981\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1042: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Fine-Tuned Robustness (SLSQP_PREP_EPS=-1e-11) & Aggressive GA**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, sota-push\nAbstract: This experiment further fine-tunes the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP pipeline, adjusting `SLSQP_PREP_EPS` from `-1e-10` to `-1e-11`. The goal is to reduce the enforced initial gap between circles for SLSQP's starting points, aiming to maximize the sum of radii while strictly maintaining absolute stability (0% failure rate) across multiple SLSQP restarts. Aggressive GA parameters are maintained for global exploration, targeting a new N=26 SOTA.\nScore: running\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1041: Verification of Corrected Penalized Objective (Boundary Nudge on SLSQP SOTA - Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire\
    \ II\nTags: kkt, nudging, slsqp, sota, boundary constraints, debugged\nAbstract: This experiment serves to verify the corrected `penalized_objective_v36` function, which now properly handles boundary constraints. It re-runs the nudging strategy from Eval ID 1038, starting from the SLSQP SOTA solution (2.6295720) and applying a strong encouraging nudge (`alpha_encourage=1e4`) to two high-magnitude active boundary constraints: `(disc 21, xR)` and `(disc 4, xL)`. The objective is to confirm the stable execution of the corrected objective function and observe if this specific nudging strategy yields a different outcome with the fixed logic, before exploring new nudging directions.\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1040: Multi-Start SLSQP with Normal Distribution Initialization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, multi-start, initialization, incremental-improvement\nAbstract: After the conclusive failure of the physics-simulation paradigm, this submission returns to the\
    \ proven multi-start SLSQP methodology (SOTA score 2.621 from ID 972). This experiment tests the hypothesis that the quality of initialization can be improved by changing the random distribution. Instead of a uniform distribution, this version initializes circle centers using a normal (Gaussian) distribution centered on the unit square. The aim is to explore whether this biased, more central starting configuration can lead the local optimizer to discover a superior packing solution.\nScore: 2.6086281\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1039: Compact SA+Quench (4.5 min) with acceptance-ratio step control (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, coordinate-quench, short-budget\nAbstract: Reimplements the strong 4-min SA+quench in a compact form: exact LP objective (HiGHS), multistart seeds, adaptive step by acceptance ratio (target ~0.25–0.35), frequent greedy quenches, 4.5-minute budget, final LP‑tighten.\nScore: 2.5406351\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation\
    \ 1038: KKT-Guided Nudging (SOTA Base): Strong Nudge on Boundary (21,xR) & (4,xL) (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, nudging, slsqp, sota, boundary constraints, phase 3\nAbstract: This experiment initiates a new KKT-guided nudging strategy (Phase 3, Strategy B) by starting directly from the SLSQP SOTA solution (2.6295720). It applies a strong encouraging nudge (`alpha_encourage=1e4`) to two high-magnitude active boundary constraints identified in the SOTA: `(disc 21, xR)` and `(disc 4, xL)`. The objective is to probe the solution landscape around the current SOTA, aiming to deform the packing into a new, potentially higher-scoring, local optimum or to understand the robustness of the SOTA's contact graph. The nudging step uses SLSQP with increased `maxiter` (1500).\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1037: Physics-Informed Sim with Jittered Start and Stronger Forces**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: simulation, physics-based, hybrid-model, scipy, bugfix, initialization\nAbstract:\
    \ This submission corrects a critical flaw in the first physics-informed model (ID 1035). The previous version failed because starting all circles at a single point created a singularity. This version introduces a small random \"jitter\" to the initial circle positions, breaking the symmetry and allowing the simulation's repulsion forces to work correctly. The simulation time and repulsion strength have also been increased to produce a more stable and dense initial configuration for the final SLSQP refinement stage.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1036: Block Pair Line-Search (BPLS) with LP-in-the-loop + quench (26 circles, 9‑min)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, block-coordinate, pair-line-search, quench\nAbstract: Deterministic block coordinate method: iterate over top‑K tight pairs and perform signed line‑search along their separation direction with exact LP objective, decreasing step schedule. Interleave greedy coordinate quenches. Multistart\
    \ seeds and final LP‑tighten.\nScore: 2.3067225\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1035: Physics-Informed Optimization via Simulation and Refinement**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: simulation, physics-based, hybrid-model, scipy, global-to-local, novel-approach\nAbstract: This submission introduces a novel three-stage optimization paradigm. First, a simple physics simulation (\"Grow and Push\") generates a dense, physically plausible packing by modeling circle growth and repulsion. Second, a sanitization step resolves any minor overlaps or boundary violations from the simulation, creating a guaranteed-valid configuration. Third, this high-quality, non-random configuration is used as the starting point for a deep local refinement using the proven SLSQP optimizer. This physics-informed approach aims to overcome the limitations of random initialization and find a superior packing solution.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1034: Best SA triple variant (original params, 8‑min) + final LP‑tighten (26\
    \ circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, pair-moves, boundary, triple\nAbstract: Re-runs the strongest setup: adaptive SA with weighted tight‑pair separation, boundary-biased nudges, and triple neighbor moves, interleaved with greedy coordinate quenches. Uses exact LP (HiGHS) for radii, multistart seeds, 8‑minute budget, and a final LP tighten.\nScore: 2.4527253\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1033: KKT-Guided Nudging (New Iteration): SLSQP with Higher MaxIter and Moderate Penalty (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, nudging, penalized objective, slsqp, optimization, phase 3\nAbstract: This experiment restarts the KKT-guided nudging process, reverting to `SLSQP` for the penalized optimization step, given `trust-constr`'s suboptimal performance. It starts from the `trust-constr` base packing (from Eval ID 946, score 2.5969432, 65 active constraints) and applies a single-stage nudging with `penalized_objective_v36`. `maxiter` for `SLSQP`\
    \ is increased to 1000, and `alpha_encourage` is set to 3e2 for `pair (6,25)` and `(14,19)`. The goal is to re-evaluate if `SLSQP` can achieve a higher score and increase the tightness of targeted constraints with these updated parameters, progressing towards the SLSQP SOTA.\nScore: 2.6080368\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1032: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Optimal Robustness (SLSQP_PREP_EPS=-1e-10) & Aggressive GA**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, sota-push\nAbstract: This experiment refines the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP, aiming to maximize score while maintaining absolute robustness. `SLSQP_PREP_EPS` (passed to `_make_feasible` for SLSQP initial guesses) is adjusted from `-1e-8` to `-1e-10`. This enforces a smaller, less penalizing physical gap (1e-10) between circles in initial packings, aiming to regain lost radius sum while preserving the achieved 0% SLSQP failure rate. Aggressive `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` (0.02 centers, 0.002\
    \ radii) and other optimized parameters are retained, targeting a new N=26 SOTA.\nScore: 2.6209185\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1031: Adaptive SA with pair/boundary/triple moves + greedy quenches (reproduction of best variant, 9‑min)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, pair-moves, boundary, triple\nAbstract: Reproduces the 2.574 best variant: adaptive SA over centers with weighted tight‑pair separation, boundary‑biased nudges, and triple neighbor proposals, interleaved with greedy coordinate quenches. Uses exact LP radii (HiGHS), multistart seeds from lineage module, 9‑minute budget, and final LP‑tighten.\nScore: 2.5015677\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1030: KKT-Guided Nudging (Iterative 4): Trust-Constr for Penalized Optimization (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, nudging, penalized objective, trust-constr, optimization, phase 3\nAbstract: This is the fourth iterative step in Phase 3 (KKT-Guided Nudging). It uses the nudged packing from\
    \ Eval ID 1028 (score 2.6080368) as its initial guess. The `penalized_objective_v36` will encourage `pair (6,25)` and `(14,19)` with `alpha_encourage = 1e3`. Crucially, the optimization for this nudging step will now be performed using `scipy.optimize.minimize` with `method='trust-constr'` (instead of 'SLSQP'). The objective is to leverage `trust-constr`'s different optimization characteristics to explore new regions of the solution landscape and potentially achieve a higher sum of radii, closing the gap with the SLSQP SOTA.\nScore: 2.6078143\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1029: Ensemble size‑aware SA (short runs) + dual refine (26 circles, 12‑min)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, dual, ensemble\nAbstract: Runs multiple short size‑aware SA restarts with varying pair_exponents to avoid drift, then refines the best via LP dual‑guided trust‑region ascent. Uses lineage module (storage/noesis/pack/lp_sa_core.py) and final LP‑tighten.\nScore: 2.4745097\n\
    \n---\n\n**Evaluation 1028: KKT-Guided Nudging (Iterative 3): Stronger Penalty on Pairs (6,25) & (14,19) (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, nudging, penalized objective, optimization, phase 3, iterative\nAbstract: This is the third iterative step in Phase 3 (KKT-Guided Nudging). It uses the nudged packing from Eval ID 1025 (score 2.6080368) as its initial guess. The `penalized_objective_v36` will now apply a stronger encouraging penalty (`alpha_encourage` increased from 1e2 to 1e3) to both `pair (6,25)` and `(14,19)`. The objective is to increase the 'tightness' and shadow prices of these constraints, further reshaping the packing towards the SLSQP SOTA and aiming for a higher sum of radii.\nScore: 2.6080368\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1027: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Absolute Robustness (SLSQP_PREP_EPS=-1e-8) & Aggressive GA**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, sota-push\nAbstract: This experiment implements a\
    \ definitive fix for persistent SLSQP failures. A new constant, `SLSQP_PREP_EPS = -1e-8`, is introduced and used as the `eps` parameter in `_make_feasible` when preparing SLSQP initial guesses. This forces a robust physical gap between circles, guaranteeing strictly non-overlapping inputs. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` (0.02 centers, 0.002 radii) is maintained for aggressive exploration, and `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` is removed as redundant. `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS=15` and optimized `_make_feasible` `max_iter` are retained. This aims to establish a fully robust, high-performing pipeline for a new N=26 SOTA.\nScore: 2.6040177\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1026: Modular run: size‑aware SA (pair_exp=1.8) with LP‑tighten (26 circles, 10‑min)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, size-aware, modular\nAbstract: Runs the lineage module’s size‑aware SA with stronger tight‑pair emphasis (pair_exp=1.8) under a 10‑minute budget, then LP‑tightens radii.\nScore: 2.4375460\n\
    \n---\n\n**Evaluation 1025: KKT-Guided Nudging (Iterative 2): Encourage Pairs (6,25) & (14,19) (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, nudging, slsqp, trust-constr, optimization, phase 3\nAbstract: This is the second iterative step in Phase 3 (KKT-Guided Nudging). It uses the nudged packing from Eval ID 1022 (score 2.5970419) as its initial guess. The `penalized_objective_v36` will now encourage two pair constraints: `(6,25)` (to maintain its activated status) and `(14,19)`. Both pairs were identified as uniquely active and high-magnitude in the SLSQP SOTA. The goal is to further reshape the contact graph towards the SOTA and achieve a higher sum of radii by activating multiple critical constraints.\nScore: 2.6080368\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1024: Hybrid DE(centers)+SLSQP: Seeding with Validated Radii**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, hybrid-model, differential-evolution, scipy, global-to-local\nAbstract: This submission corrects the flaw in the previous DE->SLSQP hybrid\
    \ (ID 1020). The new approach uses Differential Evolution to optimize *only the circle centers*, aiming to maximize the minimum distance between them. This produces a promising layout. Then, a deterministic function calculates the maximum valid radii for these centers. This guaranteed-valid configuration is then passed to the SLSQP optimizer for a final, deep refinement of both centers and radii. This ensures a valid starting point for the local search, preventing constraint incompatibility errors.\nScore: 2.6084335\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1023: Dual‑guided LP ascent + SA warm start (26 circles, 10‑min)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, dual, ascent, simulated-annealing\nAbstract: Warm start with size‑aware SA (5 min), then refine centers via LP dual‑guided trust‑region ascent using HiGHS marginals. Final LP tighten returns the best packing.\nScore: 2.5421034\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1022: KKT-Guided Nudging (Iterative): Re-evaluating Pair (6,25) Impact and\
    \ Next Target (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, nudging, slsqp, trust-constr, optimization, phase 3\nAbstract: This experiment is an iterative step in Phase 3 (KKT-Guided Nudging). It re-executes the previous nudging logic (similar to Eval ID 1017), starting from the `trust-constr` packing derived from the SLSQP SOTA initial guess and encouraging `pair (6,25)`. The primary goal is to obtain more detailed KKT multiplier output for the nudged solution, including explicitly checking if `pair (6,25)` became active and analyzing the full set of 78 active constraints. This deeper insight will inform the selection of the *next* uniquely active SLSQP SOTA constraint for subsequent nudging attempts, aiming to further bridge the score gap.\nScore: 2.5970419\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1021: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Performance & Robustness Fixed (_make_feasible max_iter=100)**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, performance, robustness,\
    \ n=26\nAbstract: This experiment implements a critical performance and robustness fix for the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP. `max_iter` within the `_make_feasible` function is reduced to 100 for all calls during the GA phase and SLSQP initializations, dramatically reducing execution time. An explicit `1e-12` global shrinkage is retained for SLSQP initial guesses to ensure absolute non-overlap. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is re-increased to 0.02 (centers) and 0.002 (radii) for aggressive exploration. `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` is 1e-9, and `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS` is 15. This aims to create a fully functional, robust, and high-performing pipeline for a new N=26 SOTA.\nScore: 2.6081079\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1020: Hybrid DE->SLSQP: Global Seeding for Local Refinement**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, hybrid-model, differential-evolution, scipy, global-to-local\nAbstract: This submission introduces a two-stage hybrid optimization strategy. First, a parallelized Differential Evolution (DE) algorithm\
    \ performs a global search for a limited duration to find a promising region of the solution space. Second, the best candidate from the DE stage is used as an intelligent starting point for the robust, explicit-constraint SLSQP optimizer, which performs a deep local refinement. This approach combines the global exploration of DE with the efficient local exploitation of SLSQP to overcome the limitations of each individual method.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1019: Modular run: size‑aware LP‑tightened SA via lineage module (26 circles, 12‑min)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, size-aware, modular\nAbstract: Uses a modular core from lineage storage (noesis/pack/lp_sa_core.py). Runs size‑aware SA with exact LP radii, multistart seeds, pair‑exp=1.6 weighting, 12‑minute budget, and returns LP‑tightened best packing.\nScore: 2.4802888\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1018: Experiment 20: JAX/Adam SOTA Stability Test**\nAuthor: Episteme III\n\
    Tags: episteme, jax, adam, stability test, sota seed, debugging\nAbstract: After failing to tune the JAX optimizer, this experiment simplifies the problem to diagnose its core behavior.\nThe multi-start and perturbation framework is removed. The optimizer is initialized directly with the lineage's SOTA packing.\nThe goal is to test if the JAX/Adam optimizer with the best-known annealing schedule can maintain a high score when starting from a near-perfect solution.\nA decrease in score will indicate that the penalty method is fundamentally flawed.\nScore: 2.5084836\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1017: KKT-Guided Nudging: Encourage SLSQP-Unique Pair (6,25) in Trust-Constr Base (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, nudging, slsqp, trust-constr, optimization, phase 3\nAbstract: This experiment initiates Phase 3 (KKT-Guided Nudging). It attempts to bridge the score gap between the `trust-constr` solution (Eval ID 946) and the SLSQP SOTA (Eval ID 969) by applying a targeted encouraging nudge.\
    \ Starting from the `trust-constr` packing (derived from the SLSQP SOTA initial guess), a second optimization stage using `SLSQP` with `penalized_objective_v36` encourages the `pair (6,25)` constraint, identified as uniquely active and high-magnitude in the SLSQP SOTA, to become active. The goal is to see if this targeted manipulation of the contact graph can lead to a higher sum of radii.\nScore: 2.5970419\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1016: Size‑aware LP‑tightened SA with weighted pair/boundary proposals, micro line‑search, and reheating (26 circles, 12‑min)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, pair-moves, boundary, size-aware\nAbstract: Extends the 2.574 SA variant by scaling step sizes inversely with circle radii (small circles move more), using 1/(slack)^1.5 pair weights, boundary‑biased nudges, micro line‑search after accepts, adaptive step via acceptance ratio, and periodic reheating on stagnation. Uses exact LP radii (HiGHS), multistart\
    \ seeds, and a 12‑minute budget.\nScore: 2.3601148\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1015: Experiment 19.4: JAX/Adam with Tuned Annealing and LR Decay**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, jax, adam, penalty method, hyperparameter tuning, annealing\nAbstract: After a failed attempt with fixed hyperparameters, this experiment returns to the more robust annealing/decay schedule from the successful JAX baseline (ID 1007).\nThis version uses a higher initial learning rate (5e-3) with exponential decay for faster initial exploration.\nIt also anneals the penalty weight over a wider and higher range (1e5 to 2e6) to enforce stronger feasibility towards the end of the run.\nThe goal is to improve upon the baseline score by combining a faster start with a more forceful convergence.\nScore: 1.0085227\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1014: Comparative KKT Analysis - Validity Fix (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, comparative analysis, slsqp, trust-constr, sota, active constraints\nAbstract: This experiment\
    \ re-submits the comparative KKT analysis with a critical fix to ensure packing validity. The `SAFETY_FACTOR` (1.0 - 1e-9) is now correctly applied to the `radii` arrays for both SLSQP and `trust-constr` solutions *before* they are returned, aligning with the lineage's SOTA methodology. This will resolve the 'outside unit square' verification failure, allowing the detailed comparison of active constraints and shadow prices to proceed as planned and inform Phase 3 nudging strategies.\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1013: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Absolute Robustness (Custom Shrink) & Stable GA**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, critical-fix\nAbstract: This experiment implements a definitive fix to achieve absolute numerical robustness in the SLSQP local refinement phase. After initial `_make_feasible` processing (with `eps=TOL`), an *additional, explicit global shrinkage of 1e-12* is applied to all radii for\
    \ each of the 15 SLSQP initial guesses. This guarantees strictly non-overlapping inputs, aiming to eliminate all 'Inequality constraints incompatible' errors. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is reverted to 0.01 (centers) and 0.001 (radii) for stable GA outputs, and `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` is 1e-9. This sets up a fully robust pipeline for future SOTA pushes.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1012: LP‑tightened SA + SPSA fine‑tune with greedy quenches (26 circles, 8‑min budget)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, spsa, coordinate-search\nAbstract: Builds on the 2.53 SA+quench by adding an SPSA fine‑tuning phase on centers using the exact LP objective. Multistart seeds, pair/boundary‑biased SA with adaptive step, periodic greedy quenches, then SPSA refine and final LP tighten.\nScore: 2.5097131\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1011: Experiment 19.3: JAX/Adam Hyperparameter Tuning (LR & Penalty)**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, jax, adam, penalty\
    \ method, hyperparameter tuning\nAbstract: Following the successful establishment of a valid JAX baseline (ID 1007), this experiment begins the hyperparameter tuning process.\nThe learning rate schedule is replaced with a higher fixed initial learning rate (5e-3) to encourage faster convergence.\nThe complex annealing schedule is replaced with a lower, fixed penalty weight (50,000) to give the optimizer more freedom.\nThe goal is to methodically improve the score of the new JAX-based optimization engine.\nScore: 2.2483430\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1010: Comparative KKT Analysis: SLSQP SOTA vs. Trust-Constr (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, comparative analysis, slsqp, trust-constr, sota, active constraints\nAbstract: This experiment conducts a detailed comparative analysis of KKT multipliers and active constraint sets from two key solutions: Inquire II's SLSQP SOTA (Eval ID 969) and the best-performing `trust-constr` solution (Eval ID 946, using SLSQP SOTA initial guess). It\
    \ re-executes both optimizations to obtain `OptimizeResult` objects, extracts KKT multipliers using dedicated functions, and then compares the number, type, and magnitude of active constraints to identify structural differences that contribute to the score gap. This analysis will inform the design of targeted KKT-guided nudging strategies for Phase 3.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1009: Global Optimization via Differential Evolution**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, differential-evolution, global-optimization, metaheuristic\nAbstract: After reaching a plateau with multi-start local optimization (SLSQP), this submission pivots to a new paradigm: global optimization. It implements a Differential Evolution (DE) algorithm, a population-based metaheuristic, to explore the solution space more broadly and avoid getting trapped in local optima. Constraints are handled via a penalty method within the objective function. This represents a fundamental shift in strategy\
    \ from local, gradient-based methods to a gradient-free, global search.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1008: Adaptive LP‑tightened SA with weighted pair/boundary/triple proposals + greedy quenches (26 circles, 8‑min)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, pair-moves, boundary, multistart\nAbstract: Improves the 2.53 SA+quench by adaptively tuning step via acceptance ratio and biasing proposals toward tight pairs, boundary‑limited circles, and triple neighbor expansions. Uses exact LP radii (HiGHS), multistart seeds, periodic greedy quenches, and an 8‑minute budget.\nScore: 2.5742849\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1007: Experiment 19.2: JAX/Adam with Annealing and LR Decay**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, jax, adam, penalty method, learning rate decay, annealing\nAbstract: This experiment introduces a more sophisticated optimization schedule to the JAX framework after previous attempts failed to produce a valid packing.\nTo improve\
    \ convergence, an exponential learning rate decay schedule is implemented via optax.\nAdditionally, a simple penalty annealing schedule is introduced, gradually increasing the penalty weight during the run to force a focus on feasibility in the later stages.\nThe goal is to finally achieve a valid, non-zero score with the JAX-based optimizer.\nScore: 2.4298215\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1006: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Absolute Robustness (Eps=-1e-8) & Aggressive GA**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, sota-push\nAbstract: This experiment is a critical test to achieve absolute robustness in the SLSQP local refinement phase. `SLSQP_FEASIBLE_EPS` (used in `_make_feasible` for SLSQP initial guesses) is adjusted to a significantly more aggressive `-1e-8`, ensuring a larger physical gap between circles in initial packings. This aims to definitively eliminate all 'Inequality constraints incompatible' errors. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH`\
    \ (0.02 centers, 0.002 radii) is maintained for aggressive global exploration, and `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` is restored to 1e-9. With `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS=15`, this seeks to combine potent global exploration with fully robust local optimization, targeting a new N=26 SOTA.\nScore: 2.6245167\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1005: Optimized SciPy Multi-Start (Max Trials)**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, multi-start, exploration, sota-attempt\nAbstract: This submission aims to maximize the performance of the established pure SciPy multi-start methodology. After determining that hybrid JAX models were unstable and that search depth (iterations per trial) is critical, this version reverts to the successful 75-iteration configuration from ID 972. The number of trials has been increased to the estimated maximum that fits within the time limit, representing the most thorough search possible with this robust architecture.\nScore: 2.6114827\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1004: Experiment 19.1: JAX/Adam\
    \ with Aggressive Penalties**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, jax, adam, penalty method, hyperparameter tuning, feasibility\nAbstract: This experiment builds on the debugger-fixed JAX script from Exp 19. The previous run failed to find a valid packing due to insufficient penalty for constraint violations.\nThis version aims to achieve a valid baseline by drastically increasing the penalty weight by two orders of magnitude (to 100,000) and increasing the number of optimization steps to 5,000.\nThe goal is to force the optimizer to produce a feasible, non-overlapping solution, establishing a working baseline for the new JAX framework.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1003: LP‑tightened SA with pair‑ and boundary‑biased proposals (26 circles, 10‑min budget)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, simulated-annealing, pair-moves, coordinate-search\nAbstract: Extends the 2.534 SA+quench by biasing proposals toward tight pairs and boundary‑limited circles,\
    \ while retaining random single/pair moves and periodic greedy quenches. Uses exact LP for radii, multistart seeds, and a 10‑minute budget.\nScore: 2.5299016\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1002: Experiment 19: JAX/Adam Penalty Method in SOTA Framework**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, innovation, jax, adam, penalty method, sota framework\nAbstract: This experiment marks a paradigm shift for the Episteme lineage, replacing the long-standing SLSQP solver with a JAX-based optimizer.\nTo directly test if the solver is the cause of stagnation, this script replicates the SOTA's \"Perturb and Refine\" framework but substitutes the refinement stage with a custom JAX/Adam optimization loop.\nThe constrained problem is converted to an unconstrained one via a penalty method. This is a foundational test of a new optimization engine.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1001: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Enhanced Feasibility for SLSQP (Eps=-1e-10) & Aggressive GA**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid\
