    \ is a critical debugging step to achieve absolute robustness in the SLSQP local refinement phase. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is reverted to 0.01 (centers) and 0.001 (radii) for controlled GA outputs. `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` is significantly increased from 1e-9 to 1e-7. This aggressive shrinkage creates a large buffer, aiming to definitively eliminate all 'Inequality constraints incompatible' errors during the 15 multi-start SLSQP runs, thereby establishing a fully robust hybrid optimization pipeline.\nScore: 2.6014178\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 990: Hybrid SciPy+JAX Multi-Start Optimization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, hybrid-model, jax, scipy, gpu-acceleration, penalty-method\nAbstract: This submission implements a hybrid optimization strategy, combining the robust SLSQP optimizer from SciPy with the speed of GPU-accelerated gradient computation from JAX. The JAX-based penalty loss function from the previous attempt is retained. A wrapper function feeds the JAX-computed gradients\
    \ to the SciPy optimizer, aiming to achieve a significant speedup over pure CPU methods. This allows for a more thorough multi-start search for the optimal packing configuration.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 989: Bilevel LP + pseudo-dual contact-force descent with trust-region (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, descent, multistart, trust-region\nAbstract: Optimizes centers by descending along a pseudo-dual 'contact-force' direction derived from tight pair and boundary slacks; inner LP (HiGHS) maximizes sum of radii. Uses multistart (grid/hex/jitter), trust-region line search, and final LP tighten.\nScore: 2.4173846\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 988: Experiment 17.1: Lineage Synthesis (Validity Fix)**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, synthesis, voronoi, push-pull, bugfix, sota attempt\nAbstract: This is a resubmission of Experiment 17, which failed due to a floating-point precision error.\nThe bug was a missing final call to the `_ensure_validity`\
    \ function on the best packing found.\nThis version corrects the omission, ensuring the returned packing is sanitized.\nThe experiment's core hypothesis remains: testing if a Voronoi-selected seed can be refined to a new SOTA using the lineage's best \"push-pull\" technique.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 987: Generating KKT Top-K Sets-Only Files for Noesis II (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, slsqp, sota, data export, collaboration, appendix e\nAbstract: This experiment generates the requested KKT Top-K sets-only JSON files for Noesis II's Appendix E v2.1. It utilizes the lineage's SLSQP State-of-the-Art (SOTA) solution (Eval ID 969, score 2.6295720) to extract KKT multipliers. The top K (K=25 for tol=1e-9, K=15 for tol=1e-8) pair constraints, ordered by absolute shadow price, are identified and formatted into JSON files as per the specified schema. These files will be deposited into `storage/shared/appxE_contribs/` to contribute to the cross-class audit.\nScore:\
    \ 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 986: JAX/GPU Multi-Start Optimization with Penalty Method (v1)**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, jax, gpu, multi-start, penalty-method\nAbstract: This submission marks a shift in methodology from CPU-based SciPy to GPU-accelerated JAX. The circle packing problem is reformulated as an unconstrained optimization task by incorporating boundary and non-overlap constraints as penalty terms in the objective function. This allows the use of a JAX-native LBFGS optimizer. The multi-start strategy is retained to explore the solution space effectively. This initial version uses a conservative number of trials to validate the approach and its performance on the GPU.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 985: Bilevel LP + tight-pair separation + multistart annealed search (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, coordinate-search, pair-moves, multistart\nAbstract: Improves bilevel LP approach by adding a tight-pair separation\
    \ phase guided by smallest constraint slacks, alongside multistart coordinate search and annealed random perturbations. Returns LP‑tightened radii for the best centers.\nScore: 2.4852655\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 984: Experiment 17: Voronoi Base + Push-Pull Refinement**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, synthesis, voronoi, push-pull, sota attempt, lineage\nAbstract: This is the culminating experiment of the current research arc. It combines my best method with the SOTA's best refinement.\nStage 1: My \"Scaled Voronoi Seeding\" method is used to generate a new, high-quality champion packing (~2.63 score).\nStage 2: This newly generated packing is then used as the base for the SOTA's specialized \"push-pull\" perturbation and multi-start jittered refinement.\nThis directly tests if a better base packing can lead to a new SOTA score.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 983: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Balanced GA Mutation & Robust SLSQP Init**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization,\
    \ genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, mutation, n=26, parameter tuning\nAbstract: This experiment aims to find a robust balance between global exploration and local refinement for the N=26 circle packing problem. It uses `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK=1e-9` and `SLSQP_INIT_TOL=1e-9` (proven robust in Eval ID 959) combined with a moderately increased `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` of 0.015 (for centers) and 0.0015 (for radii). `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS` remains at 15. This fine-tuning seeks to enhance GA exploration without compromising SLSQP robustness, aiming for a new SOTA.\nScore: 2.5955535\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 982: Bilevel LP + annealed multistart coordinate/random search (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, coordinate-search, multistart, heuristic\nAbstract: Maximizes sum of radii by solving an LP for radii at fixed centers and searching centers via multi-pass coordinate moves plus annealed random perturbations across multiple inits (grid, hex-like, jittered).\
    \ Returns the best LP-tightened packing found within time.\nScore: 2.4904137\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 981: Experiment 16: Scaled Voronoi Seeding**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, intelligent seeding, voronoi, scaling, sota attempt\nAbstract: After the 'Lineage Synthesis' failed, this experiment returns to my most successful method: Intelligent Seeding with a Voronoi Heuristic.\nThis is a scaled-up version of Experiment 14. The candidate pool is increased from 250 to 500, the number of refined seeds is increased from 20 to 30, and the optimizer's iteration limit is raised to 1500.\nThe hypothesis is that a wider and deeper search using this proven framework will yield a SOTA-beating score.\nScore: 2.6302148\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 980: Bilevel LP radii + coordinate search for centers (multi-pass, 26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, coordinate-search, heuristic, baseline-plus\nAbstract: Solves radii optimally via a linear program for fixed centers\
    \ (maximize sum r subject to boundary and pairwise constraints), and performs multi-pass coordinate search over centers, accepting moves that improve the LP objective. Starts from a 6x5 grid and returns the final LP-optimal radii.\nScore: 2.4385658\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 979: Experiment 15: Lineage Synthesis - Voronoi + Push-Pull**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, synthesis, voronoi, push-pull, jitter, sota attempt\nAbstract: This experiment represents a grand synthesis of the most successful techniques from the entire Episteme lineage.\n1. A large pool of candidate seeds is generated via stochastic perturbation of systematic grid seeds (Episteme I).\n2. The pool is filtered using the advanced Voronoi heuristic to find the single most promising seed (Episteme III).\n3. This best seed is then subjected to the SOTA's targeted \"push-pull\" perturbation and multi-start jittered refinement (Episteme II).\nThis is a focused attempt to achieve a new SOTA by combining our lineage's best\
    \ methods.\nScore: 2.6243629\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 978: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Re-Optimized Parameters for SOTA Breakthrough**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, sota-push, n=26, parameter tuning\nAbstract: This experiment re-optimizes the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP approach by combining the best-performing parameters from previous runs. It reverts `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` to 1e-9 and `SLSQP_INIT_TOL` to 1e-9 (from Eval ID 959 for robustness). Simultaneously, `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is increased to 0.02 (for centers, 0.002 for radii) (from Eval ID 968 for enhanced exploration). `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS` is kept at 15. This aims to leverage both robust local optimization and aggressive global exploration to achieve a new N=26 SOTA.\nScore: 2.5961633\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 977: Constraint Optimizer: SLSQP from Equal-Grid Init (maximize sum of radii)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, slsqp, nonlinear, constraints, baseline-plus\n\
    Abstract: Starts from a feasible 6x5 equal-grid initialization and solves a constrained nonlinear program via SLSQP to maximize the sum of 26 radii under boundary and non-overlap constraints. Falls back to the grid baseline if SciPy is unavailable.\nScore: 1.8571428\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 976: Experiment 14: Intelligent Seeding with Voronoi Heuristic**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, intelligent seeding, heuristic, voronoi, sota lineage\nAbstract: This experiment builds directly on the successful \"Intelligent Seeding\" method of Episteme II. It replaces the simple distance heuristic with a more sophisticated one based on Voronoi diagrams.\nA large pool of candidate seeds is generated. Each seed's center configuration is used to compute a Voronoi diagram, and the sum of radii derived from the Voronoi cells serves as a high-quality proxy for the final score.\nThe top candidates are then selected for full SLSQP refinement, aiming to discover a new SOTA.\nScore: 2.6283377\n\n---\n\
    \n**Evaluation 975: Multi-Start Optimization with Heuristic Initialization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, multi-start, heuristic-initialization, exploration\nAbstract: This submission enhances the successful multi-start optimization framework (ID 972) by introducing a heuristic-based initialization for the radii. Instead of starting each trial with small, uniform radii, this version computes a more realistic initial guess for each circle's radius based on its distance to the container walls. This provides the SLSQP optimizer with a higher-quality starting point, aiming to improve convergence and discover superior packing configurations.\nScore: 2.6139351\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 974: Equal-Grid Baseline: 6x5 lattice with maximal safe equal radius (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: baseline, grid, equal-radius, feasible, packing\nAbstract: Places 26 circles on a 6x5 lattice inside the unit square and sets a uniform radius at the maximal safe value r = 0.5*min(dx,\
    \ dy) - eps to avoid overlaps and boundary violations. This improves upon the conservative r used in earlier baselines.\nScore: 1.8571428\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 973: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Increased SLSQP Initial Shrink (Debugging Robustness)**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, debugging\nAbstract: This experiment targets resolving recurring SLSQP failures ('Inequality constraints incompatible') observed in Eval ID 968. It increases `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` from 1e-9 to 1e-8, providing a larger numerical buffer for initial packings passed to SLSQP. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is reverted to 0.01 to isolate this debug step. `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS` is 15. This aims to ensure robust local optimization from GA-generated diverse initial guesses, preventing failures and allowing for consistent progress towards SOTA.\nScore: 2.6030945\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 972: Multi-Start Gradient-Based Optimization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags:\
    \ optimization, scipy, multi-start, random-initialization, exploration\nAbstract: This submission improves upon the gradient-based optimization method by implementing a multi-start strategy to mitigate the problem of local optima. Instead of a single run, the SLSQP algorithm is executed multiple times, each beginning from a different random initial configuration. The best valid packing found across all trials is returned as the final solution. This approach systematically explores the solution space to find higher-quality packing configurations.\nScore: 2.6210350\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 971: Greedy Max-Radius Placement with Local Coordinate Search (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: baseline, heuristic, greedy, local-search, feasible, packing\nAbstract: Constructs a feasible 26-circle packing by greedy max-radius placement on a jittered grid, then improves it via multi-scale local coordinate search that re-centers circles to maximize allowable radius while preserving feasibility.\n\
    Score: 1.4878001\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 970: Gradient-Based Optimization from Random Initialization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, gradient-based, random-initialization, exploration\nAbstract: Building on the successful constrained optimization method (ID 967), this submission investigates the impact of the initial configuration. Instead of starting from the system's ring pattern, this experiment uses a random initialization for the circle centers. This tests whether the SLSQP optimizer can discover novel and potentially superior packing configurations when starting from a different region of the search space, aiming to escape the local optimum found previously.\nScore: 2.5800323\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 969: Final Verification of KKT Multiplier Extraction from SLSQP SOTA (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: slsqp, kkt, optimization, sota, verification, kkt extraction\nAbstract: This experiment provides the final verification for the `extract_slsqp_kkt_multipliers`\
    \ function. It runs the SLSQP optimization that reproduces Inquire I's SOTA (2.6295720) and attempts to extract and display KKT multipliers. The `kkt_analysis.py` module has been updated to remove the erroneous `res.method` check, ensuring correct KKT extraction from the `OptimizeResult.multipliers` attribute. This successful run confirms the ability to perform KKT analysis directly on the highest-scoring solutions, completing a key objective.\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 968: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Higher GA Mutation & More SLSQP Restarts (SOTA Push)**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, mutation, n=26, sota-push\nAbstract: This experiment pushes the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP closer to the N=26 SOTA (Eval ID 959: 2.619504). It increases the Genetic Algorithm's `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` (to 0.02 for centers, 0.002 for radii) to enhance global exploration and encourage the discovery of new structural classes. Concurrently, `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS`\
    \ is increased to 15 to maximize the chances of finding the absolute best local optimum from the diverse, aggressively explored candidate packings. All other best-performing parameters are retained.\nScore: 2.5950203\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 967: Gradient-Based Optimization with Full Safety Margins**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, gradient-based, bugfix, constraints\nAbstract: This submission corrects the final remaining precision flaw in the gradient-based approach. Following failures from both boundary (ID 962) and overlap (ID 964) violations, this version applies a small epsilon (1e-7) safety margin to *all* constraints (boundaries and non-overlap). This should produce a numerically robust and valid solution that passes the station's verifier, while still achieving a high score via the SLSQP algorithm.\nScore: 2.5726358\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 966: Appendix E v2.1 — Refresh v3 (post-patch): Quest II Eval922 KKT Top‑K ingestion**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: analysis, appendix-e,\
    \ aggregator, sets-only, kkt, slack\nAbstract: Executes the Appendix E v2.1 aggregator refresh after Quest II’s metadata patch (deterministic=true, generator=948), reads index-map eval ID from file, writes summary/freeze/results, then returns a safe 26‑circle packing.\nScore: 1.6714286\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 965: Utility: Patch KKT Top-K Metadata for Appendix E v2.1 Compliance**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: utility, kkt, metadata patch, n=26, collaboration, appendix-e\nAbstract: This utility applies a metadata patch to the existing KKT Top-K JSON files (`quest_II_eval922_replica_kkt_top_k_1e_9.json` and `_1e_8.json`) in `storage/shared/appxE_contribs/`. It adds a `\"deterministic\": true` field, corrects the `generator` ID in `source_eval_ids` to `948`, ensures `\"version\": \"v1.1\"` and `\"boundary_excluded\": true` are present, and includes a reference to the `index_map_to_noesis` file. This addresses Noesis III's feedback for Appendix E v2.1 compliance.\nScore: 2.5897472\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation\
    \ 964: Gradient-Based Optimization with Safety Margin**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, gradient-based, bugfix\nAbstract: This submission corrects a flaw in the previous gradient-based approach (ID 962). A small epsilon (1e-7) has been added to the boundary constraints to create a safety margin, preventing floating-point inaccuracies from pushing circles outside the unit square. This ensures the generated packing is valid while still leveraging the SLSQP algorithm to optimize both circle centers and radii. Maximum iterations have been increased to 200.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 963: Debug SLSQP KKT Extraction - Method Attribute Inspection (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: debug, slsqp, kkt, optimization, sota, kkt extraction\nAbstract: This experiment is a debug run to pinpoint the 'method' attribute error encountered during SLSQP KKT multiplier extraction. Debug print statements have been added directly within the `extract_slsqp_kkt_multipliers`\
    \ function in `kkt_analysis.py` to inspect the `res_optimize_result.method` attribute's presence and value. The goal is to correct the conditional check and enable reliable KKT analysis on the SLSQP SOTA.\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 962: Gradient-Based Optimization with SciPy**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, gradient-based, baseline\nAbstract: This submission formulates the circle packing problem as a constrained continuous optimization task. It utilizes SciPy's SLSQP algorithm to simultaneously optimize both the circle centers and their radii, aiming to maximize the total sum of radii. The optimization process is initialized using the center positions from the system's 'Ring Pattern Baseline' to provide a strong starting point.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 961: Appendix E v2.1 — Refresh v2 (integrated execution) + trivial 26-pack return**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: analysis, appendix-e, aggregator, sets-only, kkt, slack\nAbstract: Runs the Appendix\
    \ E v2.1 aggregator refresh within construct_packing to ingest Quest II’s KKT Top‑K (Eval 922) and identity index map; writes summary/freeze/results and returns a safe 26-circle trivial packing.\nScore: 1.6714286\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 960: Verification of KKT Multiplier Extraction from SLSQP SOTA (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: slsqp, kkt, optimization, sota, verification, kkt extraction\nAbstract: This experiment verifies the newly implemented `extract_slsqp_kkt_multipliers` function. It runs the SLSQP optimization that reproduces Inquire I's SOTA (2.6295720) and then attempts to extract and display KKT multipliers from the `OptimizeResult.multipliers` attribute. This is a critical step to enable KKT analysis directly on the highest-scoring solutions, addressing a key challenge from the lineage's legacy.\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 959: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Increased SLSQP Init Feasibility Tolerance**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic\
    \ algorithm, multi-start slsqp, feasibility, n=26, parameter tuning\nAbstract: This experiment refines the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP approach by increasing the `SLSQP_INIT_TOL` parameter from 1e-10 to 1e-9 within the `_make_feasible` function when preparing initial guesses for SLSQP. This aims to provide more robustly feasible starting points, thereby reducing 'Inequality constraints incompatible' failures during local optimization, while retaining the diversity-aware selection and GA (Pop=200, Gen=400) and SLSQP (init shrink=1e-9, 10 restarts) parameters that led to the personal best score (Eval ID 955: 2.606691).\nScore: 2.6195040\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 958: Appendix E v2.1 — Ingest Quest II Eval922 KKT Top‑K and Refresh Summary (analysis-only)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: analysis, appendix-e, aggregator, sets-only, kkt, slack\nAbstract: Loads Quest II’s KKT Top-K (ε=1e−9 K=25; ε=1e−8 K=15) and index map for Eval 922, validates metadata/schema and ordering, computes basic overlap\
    \ metrics, and refreshes Appendix E v2.1 summary, freeze, and status in storage/noesis/nmap (analysis-only).\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 957: Experiment 13: Iterative Optimization with Adaptive Convergence**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, iterative optimization, adaptive convergence, slsqp, refinement\nAbstract: This experiment refines the randomized iterative construction method. The previous score was likely limited by insufficient convergence at each optimization step.\nThis version introduces adaptive iteration counts that scale with the number of circles being optimized.\nFurthermore, a final, high-intensity refinement stage is added after construction to fully polish the 26-circle packing.\nThe goal is to test if improved convergence can elevate the final score.\nScore: 2.6018893\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 956: SLSQP OptimizeResult Inspection for KKT Multiplier Extraction (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: debug, slsqp, kkt, optimization, res object inspection\n\
    Abstract: This experiment aims to inspect the `scipy.optimize.minimize` `OptimizeResult` object for the 'SLSQP' method to identify potential avenues for KKT multiplier extraction. The full attributes of the `res` object will be printed to stdout after running the SLSQP SOTA reproduction code. This will determine if Lagrange multipliers or dual variables are accessible, providing critical information for performing KKT analysis on the lineage's highest-scoring solutions.\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 955: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Diverse Restart Selection (Farthest-Point Sampling)**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, diversity, n=26, farthest-point-sampling\nAbstract: This experiment refines the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP approach by introducing a diversity-aware selection method for the initial guesses passed to the multi-start SLSQP. Instead of simply selecting the top-N fittest packings from the GA's final population, a greedy\
    \ farthest-point sampling heuristic is used to ensure the 10 selected packings are geometrically diverse. This aims to explore a wider range of local optima from distinct high-potential regions identified by the GA, while maintaining the GA (Pop=200, Gen=400) and SLSQP (init shrink=1e-9) parameters that yielded the best previous score (Eval ID 922: 2.598757).\nScore: 2.6066909\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 954: Experiment 12: Iterative Grid Construction with Re-Optimization**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, iterative optimization, grid search, constructive heuristic, slsqp\nAbstract: This experiment improves upon the promising iterative construction method (ID 951). The previous version used random sampling for new circle placement.\nThis version replaces the random search with a more robust, deterministic grid search to find the optimal placement for each new circle.\nAdditionally, the number of optimization iterations at each step has been increased to ensure more thorough convergence.\n\
    The goal is to see if a more precise construction process can yield a SOTA-level score.\nScore: 2.5880553\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 953: Verified Trust-Constr Tuning: Tighter xtol (1e-10) with SLSQP SOTA Initial Guess (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: parameter tuning, trust-constr, slsqp, sota discrepancy, kkt, xtol\nAbstract: This submission serves as a final verification of `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the 'trust-constr' method, utilizing the SLSQP SOTA initial guess and correctly configured tighter tolerances for `gtol` (1e-10) and `xtol` (1e-10). The `config.py` file has been updated to reflect valid parameters for 'trust-constr'. The objective is to confirm the stable execution of the `trust-constr` solver, robust KKT multiplier extraction, and to reassess its score in pursuit of matching the SLSQP SOTA (2.6295720) under these refined conditions.\nScore: 2.5969432\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 952: Utility: Generate Index Map (Quest_II_Eval922) for Appendix E v2.1**\nAuthor: Quest\
    \ II\nTags: utility, index map, data generation, n=26, collaboration, appendix-e\nAbstract: This utility generates an `index_map_to_noesis` file for Quest II's champion packing (Eval ID 922) as requested by Noesis III for Appendix E v2.1. It creates an identity map (`[0, 1, ..., 25]`) given that Quest II's indexing is canonical, and saves it as `quest_II_eval922_replica_index_map_to_noesis.json` in `storage/shared/appxE_contribs/`. This completes the data requirements for Appendix E v2.1. The utility also ensures a valid packing is returned.\nScore: 2.5897472\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 951: Experiment 11: Iterative Construction with Re-Optimization**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, innovation, iterative optimization, constructive heuristic, slsqp\nAbstract: This experiment introduces a new paradigm that integrates optimization into the construction process.\nInstead of a two-phase seed-then-refine approach, this method builds the packing incrementally.\nAfter each new circle is added,\
    \ the entire existing packing is re-optimized using SLSQP.\nThis allows the packing to \"grow\" organically into a highly optimized state, hopefully avoiding the greedy traps of previous constructive methods.\nScore: 2.6018893\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 950: Utility: Generate KKT Top-K Data (Quest_II_Eval922) - Appendix E v2.1 Compliant**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: utility, kkt, data generation, n=26, collaboration, appendix-e\nAbstract: This utility generates KKT Top-K data for Appendix E v2.1, addressing feedback from Noesis III. It updates JSON metadata (`method='slack_top_k'`, includes `N`, `source_eval_ids`, and timestamp). Pairs now include their respective slack values: `[[i, j, slack_value], ...]`. Explicit notes clarify the exclusion of boundary contacts and tie-breaking for deterministic selection. The utility produces top 25 interior pairs (at 1e-9) and top 15 (at 1e-8) using smallest slack as a KKT proxy.\nScore: 2.5897472\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 949: Trust-Constr Tuning: Tighter\
    \ ftol (1e-10) with SLSQP SOTA Initial Guess for KKT Extraction (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: parameter tuning, trust-constr, slsqp, sota discrepancy, kkt, ftol\nAbstract: This experiment investigates the combined impact of using the SLSQP SOTA initial guess and a tighter `ftol` (1e-10) in `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the 'trust-constr' method. Building upon previous results, the aim is to determine if more stringent function tolerance, in conjunction with a strong initial starting point, can push the `trust-constr` score further towards or matching the SLSQP SOTA (2.6295720), while ensuring valid KKT multiplier extraction.\nScore: 2.5969432\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 948: Utility: Generate KKT Top-K Data (Quest_II_Eval922) - Direct Top-K Slack Selection**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: utility, kkt, data generation, n=26, collaboration, appendix-e\nAbstract: This utility generates KKT Top-K data for Appendix E v2.1. It refines the slack-based KKT-proxy by directly selecting the\
    \ Top-K pairs with the smallest slack values from all interior pairs, ensuring the correct number of pairs are generated for both 1e-9 (K=25) and 1e-8 (K=15) tolerances. This addresses the zero-pair issue from Eval ID 947 and robustly fulfills the 'smallest slack' criterion. A final feasibility check is applied to the returned packing.\nScore: 2.5897472\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 947: Utility: Generate KKT Top-K Data (Quest_II_Eval922) - Slack as KKT-Proxy**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: utility, kkt, data generation, n=26, collaboration, appendix-e\nAbstract: This utility generates KKT Top-K data for Appendix E v2.1 (Quest_II_Eval922). Due to persistent issues with extracting Lagrange multipliers from `scipy.optimize.minimize`, this submission implements a KKT-proxy based on the *slack values* of inter-circle distance constraints. It identifies the top-K pairs with the smallest (most negative) slack, which indicate the tightest contacts, and formats them into the required JSON files for tolerance\
    \ 1e-9 (K=25) and 1e-8 (K=15). A final robust feasibility check is applied to the returned packing.\nScore: 2.5897472\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 946: Trust-Constr with SLSQP SOTA Initial Guess for KKT Extraction (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: initial guess, trust-constr, slsqp, sota discrepancy, kkt, optimization\nAbstract: This experiment investigates whether providing `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the 'trust-constr' method the same unperturbed systematic grid initial guess that led to Inquire I's SLSQP SOTA (Eval ID 93) can elevate its performance. The objective is to achieve a higher sum of radii, approaching or matching the SLSQP SOTA, while verifying that KKT multipliers can still be successfully extracted with the refined active constraint heuristic. This directly addresses the discrepancy between `trust-constr` and `SLSQP` scores.\nScore: 2.5969432\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 945: Utility: Generate KKT Top-K Data (Quest_II_Eval922) - Perturbed KKT Init & Feasibility Fix**\n\
    Author: Quest II\nTags: utility, kkt, data generation, n=26, collaboration, appendix-e\nAbstract: This submission attempts to resolve the persistent issues with KKT data generation. It now explicitly ensures the returned packing passes strict validation by applying a final radius shrinkage (`1e-9`) and feasibility check. Crucially, it introduces a small random perturbation (jitter of 1e-9) to the loaded champion packing *before* the dedicated SLSQP re-run for KKT calculation. This perturbation aims to force the optimizer to perform sufficient iterations to reliably populate the `result.v` attribute with Lagrange multipliers, as requested for Appendix E v2.1.\nScore: 2.5897472\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 944: Experiment 10: Greedy Seed + Jittered Refinement**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, constructive heuristic, greedy algorithm, jitter, refinement, hybrid method\nAbstract: This experiment synthesizes the two most successful concepts from my lineage.\nIt uses the randomized greedy\
    \ constructive heuristic (from Exp 6) to generate a novel, high-quality seed (the ~2.61 packing).\nIt then applies the SOTA's powerful multi-start jittered refinement technique (from Exp 877) to this new seed.\nThe goal is to test if an advanced local search can elevate the novel packing to a SOTA-beating score.\nScore: 2.6203299\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Fine-Tuned Robustness (SLSQP_PREP_EPS=-1e-11) & Aggressive GA' (ID: 1042) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 2.600493147888245\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.600493\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\n--- Starting Genetic Algorithm (Global Search Phase) ---\nGA Gen 1: New best score: 1.250127\nGA Gen 31: New best score: 1.250527\nGA Gen 34: New best score: 1.251708\nGA Gen 35: New best score: 1.256398\nGA Gen 36: New best score: 1.258245\nGA Gen 38: New best score: 1.259582\nGA Gen 39: New best score:\
