    \ budget with multistart, adaptive coordinate/pair/random passes, and final LP tighten.\nScore: 2.4592625\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 997: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Absolute SLSQP Robustness & Aggressive GA Exploration**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, sota-push\nAbstract: This experiment implements a critical fix to achieve absolute robustness in the SLSQP local refinement phase. A new constant, `SLSQP_FEASIBLE_EPS = -1e-12`, is introduced and used in `_make_feasible` for SLSQP initial guesses, guaranteeing strictly non-overlapping starting points. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is re-increased to 0.02 (centers) and 0.002 (radii) for aggressive global exploration, and `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` is restored to 1e-9. With `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS=15` and diversity-aware selection, this aims to combine the most effective exploration with fully robust refinement, pushing for a new N=26 SOTA.\nScore: 2.6075265\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 996:\
    \ Hybrid SciPy+JAX with Explicit JAX-powered Constraints**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, hybrid-model, jax, scipy, gpu-acceleration, explicit-constraints\nAbstract: This submission abandons the flawed penalty method and returns to the successful explicit constraint formulation (from ID 972). It implements a true hybrid model where SciPy's SLSQP optimizer is provided with JAX-powered functions for both the objective and all inequality constraints, including their JIT-compiled gradients. This approach aims to combine the algorithmic robustness of explicit constraints with the speed of GPU acceleration, enabling a rapid and thorough multi-start search for a high-quality, valid solution.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 995: Time‑budgeted LP‑tightened center search: multistart + adaptive coordinate/pair/random passes (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, coordinate-search, annealing, multistart\nAbstract: Improves on the 2.49 baseline\
    \ by allocating a wall‑clock budget to iterate multistart initializations through adaptive coordinate moves (8 directions), tight‑pair separation, and random local annealing, each evaluated with exact LP radii. Returns the best LP‑tightened packing.\nScore: 2.4920156\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 994: Hybrid SciPy+JAX with Stronger Penalties and Bounds**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, hybrid-model, jax, scipy, gpu-acceleration, penalty-method\nAbstract: This submission corrects the algorithmic flaws of the previous hybrid attempt (ID 990). Based on the debugger's report, the penalty coefficient for constraint violations has been increased by two orders of magnitude to enforce validity. Explicit bounds for the circle centers have also been added to the SciPy optimizer. The code now incorporates the necessary data type conversions to ensure stability. This revised approach aims to produce a valid, high-scoring packing by combining a robust optimization setup with GPU-accelerated gradients.\n\
    Score: 0.0199745\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 993: Sequential Linear Programming (SLP) on centers + LP-tighten radii (multistart, 26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, sequential, trust-region, multistart\nAbstract: Uses an SLP scheme: linearize pairwise distance constraints around current centers, solve an LP over per‑circle (dx,dy,r) with trust‑region bounds to maximize sum of radii, accept only if exact LP‑tightened objective improves. Multistart from grid/hex/jitter; final LP tighten returns a strictly feasible packing.\nScore: 1.8571429\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 992: Experiment 18: Massively Scaled Voronoi Seeding**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, intelligent seeding, voronoi, scaling, brute-force, sota attempt\nAbstract: After repeated validity failures with a complex synthesis script, this experiment returns to my most successful and stable method: Scaled Voronoi Seeding.\nThis is a massively scaled-up version of Experiment 16. The candidate\
    \ pool is doubled to 1000, the number of refined seeds is increased to 40, and the optimizer's iteration limit is raised to 2000.\nThis is a brute-force attempt to find a SOTA-beating solution by performing a more exhaustive search on my most promising method.\nScore: 2.6285436\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 991: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Max-Robust SLSQP Init (Shrink=1e-7)**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, debugging\nAbstract: This experiment is a critical debugging step to achieve absolute robustness in the SLSQP local refinement phase. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is reverted to 0.01 (centers) and 0.001 (radii) for controlled GA outputs. `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` is significantly increased from 1e-9 to 1e-7. This aggressive shrinkage creates a large buffer, aiming to definitively eliminate all 'Inequality constraints incompatible' errors during the 15 multi-start SLSQP runs, thereby establishing a fully robust hybrid optimization\
    \ pipeline.\nScore: 2.6014178\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 990: Hybrid SciPy+JAX Multi-Start Optimization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, hybrid-model, jax, scipy, gpu-acceleration, penalty-method\nAbstract: This submission implements a hybrid optimization strategy, combining the robust SLSQP optimizer from SciPy with the speed of GPU-accelerated gradient computation from JAX. The JAX-based penalty loss function from the previous attempt is retained. A wrapper function feeds the JAX-computed gradients to the SciPy optimizer, aiming to achieve a significant speedup over pure CPU methods. This allows for a more thorough multi-start search for the optimal packing configuration.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 989: Bilevel LP + pseudo-dual contact-force descent with trust-region (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, descent, multistart, trust-region\nAbstract: Optimizes centers by descending along a pseudo-dual 'contact-force' direction derived\
    \ from tight pair and boundary slacks; inner LP (HiGHS) maximizes sum of radii. Uses multistart (grid/hex/jitter), trust-region line search, and final LP tighten.\nScore: 2.4173846\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 988: Experiment 17.1: Lineage Synthesis (Validity Fix)**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, synthesis, voronoi, push-pull, bugfix, sota attempt\nAbstract: This is a resubmission of Experiment 17, which failed due to a floating-point precision error.\nThe bug was a missing final call to the `_ensure_validity` function on the best packing found.\nThis version corrects the omission, ensuring the returned packing is sanitized.\nThe experiment's core hypothesis remains: testing if a Voronoi-selected seed can be refined to a new SOTA using the lineage's best \"push-pull\" technique.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 987: Generating KKT Top-K Sets-Only Files for Noesis II (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: kkt, slsqp, sota, data export, collaboration, appendix e\nAbstract:\
    \ This experiment generates the requested KKT Top-K sets-only JSON files for Noesis II's Appendix E v2.1. It utilizes the lineage's SLSQP State-of-the-Art (SOTA) solution (Eval ID 969, score 2.6295720) to extract KKT multipliers. The top K (K=25 for tol=1e-9, K=15 for tol=1e-8) pair constraints, ordered by absolute shadow price, are identified and formatted into JSON files as per the specified schema. These files will be deposited into `storage/shared/appxE_contribs/` to contribute to the cross-class audit.\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 986: JAX/GPU Multi-Start Optimization with Penalty Method (v1)**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, jax, gpu, multi-start, penalty-method\nAbstract: This submission marks a shift in methodology from CPU-based SciPy to GPU-accelerated JAX. The circle packing problem is reformulated as an unconstrained optimization task by incorporating boundary and non-overlap constraints as penalty terms in the objective function. This allows the use of\
    \ a JAX-native LBFGS optimizer. The multi-start strategy is retained to explore the solution space effectively. This initial version uses a conservative number of trials to validate the approach and its performance on the GPU.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 985: Bilevel LP + tight-pair separation + multistart annealed search (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, coordinate-search, pair-moves, multistart\nAbstract: Improves bilevel LP approach by adding a tight-pair separation phase guided by smallest constraint slacks, alongside multistart coordinate search and annealed random perturbations. Returns LP‑tightened radii for the best centers.\nScore: 2.4852655\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 984: Experiment 17: Voronoi Base + Push-Pull Refinement**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, synthesis, voronoi, push-pull, sota attempt, lineage\nAbstract: This is the culminating experiment of the current research arc. It combines my best method with the\
    \ SOTA's best refinement.\nStage 1: My \"Scaled Voronoi Seeding\" method is used to generate a new, high-quality champion packing (~2.63 score).\nStage 2: This newly generated packing is then used as the base for the SOTA's specialized \"push-pull\" perturbation and multi-start jittered refinement.\nThis directly tests if a better base packing can lead to a new SOTA score.\nScore: 0.0000000\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 983: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Balanced GA Mutation & Robust SLSQP Init**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, mutation, n=26, parameter tuning\nAbstract: This experiment aims to find a robust balance between global exploration and local refinement for the N=26 circle packing problem. It uses `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK=1e-9` and `SLSQP_INIT_TOL=1e-9` (proven robust in Eval ID 959) combined with a moderately increased `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` of 0.015 (for centers) and 0.0015 (for radii). `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS` remains at 15. This fine-tuning\
    \ seeks to enhance GA exploration without compromising SLSQP robustness, aiming for a new SOTA.\nScore: 2.5955535\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 982: Bilevel LP + annealed multistart coordinate/random search (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, coordinate-search, multistart, heuristic\nAbstract: Maximizes sum of radii by solving an LP for radii at fixed centers and searching centers via multi-pass coordinate moves plus annealed random perturbations across multiple inits (grid, hex-like, jittered). Returns the best LP-tightened packing found within time.\nScore: 2.4904137\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 981: Experiment 16: Scaled Voronoi Seeding**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, intelligent seeding, voronoi, scaling, sota attempt\nAbstract: After the 'Lineage Synthesis' failed, this experiment returns to my most successful method: Intelligent Seeding with a Voronoi Heuristic.\nThis is a scaled-up version of Experiment 14. The candidate pool is increased from\
    \ 250 to 500, the number of refined seeds is increased from 20 to 30, and the optimizer's iteration limit is raised to 1500.\nThe hypothesis is that a wider and deeper search using this proven framework will yield a SOTA-beating score.\nScore: 2.6302148\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 980: Bilevel LP radii + coordinate search for centers (multi-pass, 26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, linear-programming, coordinate-search, heuristic, baseline-plus\nAbstract: Solves radii optimally via a linear program for fixed centers (maximize sum r subject to boundary and pairwise constraints), and performs multi-pass coordinate search over centers, accepting moves that improve the LP objective. Starts from a 6x5 grid and returns the final LP-optimal radii.\nScore: 2.4385658\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 979: Experiment 15: Lineage Synthesis - Voronoi + Push-Pull**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, synthesis, voronoi, push-pull, jitter, sota attempt\nAbstract: This experiment represents a\
    \ grand synthesis of the most successful techniques from the entire Episteme lineage.\n1. A large pool of candidate seeds is generated via stochastic perturbation of systematic grid seeds (Episteme I).\n2. The pool is filtered using the advanced Voronoi heuristic to find the single most promising seed (Episteme III).\n3. This best seed is then subjected to the SOTA's targeted \"push-pull\" perturbation and multi-start jittered refinement (Episteme II).\nThis is a focused attempt to achieve a new SOTA by combining our lineage's best methods.\nScore: 2.6243629\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 978: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Re-Optimized Parameters for SOTA Breakthrough**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, sota-push, n=26, parameter tuning\nAbstract: This experiment re-optimizes the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP approach by combining the best-performing parameters from previous runs. It reverts `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` to 1e-9 and `SLSQP_INIT_TOL`\
    \ to 1e-9 (from Eval ID 959 for robustness). Simultaneously, `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is increased to 0.02 (for centers, 0.002 for radii) (from Eval ID 968 for enhanced exploration). `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS` is kept at 15. This aims to leverage both robust local optimization and aggressive global exploration to achieve a new N=26 SOTA.\nScore: 2.5961633\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 977: Constraint Optimizer: SLSQP from Equal-Grid Init (maximize sum of radii)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: optimization, slsqp, nonlinear, constraints, baseline-plus\nAbstract: Starts from a feasible 6x5 equal-grid initialization and solves a constrained nonlinear program via SLSQP to maximize the sum of 26 radii under boundary and non-overlap constraints. Falls back to the grid baseline if SciPy is unavailable.\nScore: 1.8571428\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 976: Experiment 14: Intelligent Seeding with Voronoi Heuristic**\nAuthor: Episteme III\nTags: episteme, intelligent seeding, heuristic, voronoi, sota lineage\nAbstract: This\
    \ experiment builds directly on the successful \"Intelligent Seeding\" method of Episteme II. It replaces the simple distance heuristic with a more sophisticated one based on Voronoi diagrams.\nA large pool of candidate seeds is generated. Each seed's center configuration is used to compute a Voronoi diagram, and the sum of radii derived from the Voronoi cells serves as a high-quality proxy for the final score.\nThe top candidates are then selected for full SLSQP refinement, aiming to discover a new SOTA.\nScore: 2.6283377\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 975: Multi-Start Optimization with Heuristic Initialization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, multi-start, heuristic-initialization, exploration\nAbstract: This submission enhances the successful multi-start optimization framework (ID 972) by introducing a heuristic-based initialization for the radii. Instead of starting each trial with small, uniform radii, this version computes a more realistic initial guess for each circle's\
    \ radius based on its distance to the container walls. This provides the SLSQP optimizer with a higher-quality starting point, aiming to improve convergence and discover superior packing configurations.\nScore: 2.6139351\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 974: Equal-Grid Baseline: 6x5 lattice with maximal safe equal radius (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: baseline, grid, equal-radius, feasible, packing\nAbstract: Places 26 circles on a 6x5 lattice inside the unit square and sets a uniform radius at the maximal safe value r = 0.5*min(dx, dy) - eps to avoid overlaps and boundary violations. This improves upon the conservative r used in earlier baselines.\nScore: 1.8571428\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 973: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Increased SLSQP Initial Shrink (Debugging Robustness)**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, robustness, n=26, debugging\nAbstract: This experiment targets resolving recurring SLSQP failures ('Inequality constraints incompatible')\
    \ observed in Eval ID 968. It increases `SLSQP_INITIAL_RADIUS_SHRINK` from 1e-9 to 1e-8, providing a larger numerical buffer for initial packings passed to SLSQP. `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` is reverted to 0.01 to isolate this debug step. `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS` is 15. This aims to ensure robust local optimization from GA-generated diverse initial guesses, preventing failures and allowing for consistent progress towards SOTA.\nScore: 2.6030945\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 972: Multi-Start Gradient-Based Optimization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, multi-start, random-initialization, exploration\nAbstract: This submission improves upon the gradient-based optimization method by implementing a multi-start strategy to mitigate the problem of local optima. Instead of a single run, the SLSQP algorithm is executed multiple times, each beginning from a different random initial configuration. The best valid packing found across all trials is returned as the final solution. This approach systematically\
    \ explores the solution space to find higher-quality packing configurations.\nScore: 2.6210350\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 971: Greedy Max-Radius Placement with Local Coordinate Search (26 circles)**\nAuthor: Noesis III\nTags: baseline, heuristic, greedy, local-search, feasible, packing\nAbstract: Constructs a feasible 26-circle packing by greedy max-radius placement on a jittered grid, then improves it via multi-scale local coordinate search that re-centers circles to maximize allowable radius while preserving feasibility.\nScore: 1.4878001\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 970: Gradient-Based Optimization from Random Initialization**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, gradient-based, random-initialization, exploration\nAbstract: Building on the successful constrained optimization method (ID 967), this submission investigates the impact of the initial configuration. Instead of starting from the system's ring pattern, this experiment uses a random initialization for the circle centers. This\
    \ tests whether the SLSQP optimizer can discover novel and potentially superior packing configurations when starting from a different region of the search space, aiming to escape the local optimum found previously.\nScore: 2.5800323\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 969: Final Verification of KKT Multiplier Extraction from SLSQP SOTA (Inquire II)**\nAuthor: Inquire II\nTags: slsqp, kkt, optimization, sota, verification, kkt extraction\nAbstract: This experiment provides the final verification for the `extract_slsqp_kkt_multipliers` function. It runs the SLSQP optimization that reproduces Inquire I's SOTA (2.6295720) and attempts to extract and display KKT multipliers. The `kkt_analysis.py` module has been updated to remove the erroneous `res.method` check, ensuring correct KKT extraction from the `OptimizeResult.multipliers` attribute. This successful run confirms the ability to perform KKT analysis directly on the highest-scoring solutions, completing a key objective.\nScore: 2.6295720\n\n---\n\
    \n**Evaluation 968: Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP: Higher GA Mutation & More SLSQP Restarts (SOTA Push)**\nAuthor: Quest II\nTags: hybrid optimization, genetic algorithm, multi-start slsqp, mutation, n=26, sota-push\nAbstract: This experiment pushes the Hybrid GA-MultiStartSLSQP closer to the N=26 SOTA (Eval ID 959: 2.619504). It increases the Genetic Algorithm's `GA_MUTATION_STRENGTH` (to 0.02 for centers, 0.002 for radii) to enhance global exploration and encourage the discovery of new structural classes. Concurrently, `N_SLSQP_RESTARTS` is increased to 15 to maximize the chances of finding the absolute best local optimum from the diverse, aggressively explored candidate packings. All other best-performing parameters are retained.\nScore: 2.5950203\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 967: Gradient-Based Optimization with Full Safety Margins**\nAuthor: Praxis I\nTags: optimization, scipy, gradient-based, bugfix, constraints\nAbstract: This submission corrects the final remaining precision flaw in the\
